# Listening to Handel's Water Music (1717): a piece drawn as a listening map, with letters for the
# tunes that return, the family that has the tune and the loudness marks, and the hornpipe's own
# tune played from its score, with the canal town's band on a narrowboat.
lesson music-listening-to-water-music v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Listening to Handel's Water Music"
  goal "Follow a piece on a listening map, telling which tune returns, who plays it and how loud it is."

  look {
    scene 36x22 {
      column all space=1 align=start at=canvas(1, 1) {
        narrowboat boat windows=4 pots=2
        listenmap map title="Handel, Water Music: Alla Hornpipe" sections=[0, 0, 1, 0] bars=[] tune=[-1, -1, -1, -1] loud=[-1, -1, -1, -1] tempo=[0, 0, 0, 0] count=0 ask=-1
      }
    }
    say """
      On 17 July 1717 King George I sailed up the River Thames in London on a royal barge, and about
      fifty musicians played beside him on another barge. George Frideric Handel wrote the music for
      them, and the king liked it so much that it was played at least three times that night. It is
      called the Water Music. One of its pieces, the Alla Hornpipe, is drawn here as a listening
      map, a strip read from left to right in the order you hear it. Each tune gets a letter and a
      shape: tune A is played twice, then a new tune, B, and then A comes back. Under a section a
      map can draw the family of instruments that has the tune and a mark for how loud it is.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.water-returns f=1 easy-f=0 hard-f=2
    level easy {
      worked music.water-family f=0 level=medium
    }
    show music.water-family f=2 easy-f=1 hard-f=3
    show music.water-loud f=1 easy-f=0 hard-f=2
    show music.water-theme
    show music.water-bars f=1 easy-f=0 hard-f=2
    show music.water-returns f=2 easy-f=1 hard-f=3
    show music.water-loud f=2 easy-f=1 hard-f=3
    show music.water-bars f=2 easy-f=1 hard-f=3
    level hard {
      show music.water-family f=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.water-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-q=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.water-clues v=0 a=1 easy-a=0 hard-v=1
  }
  remember {
    say "On a listening map the same tune always gets the same letter, whoever plays it and however loud."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a piece of music has a plan you can follow while you listen, drawn as a strip
    of shapes with a letter for each tune, the family of instruments that has the tune, and how loud
    it is. The wrong idea to watch for is giving a section a new letter because the instruments or
    the loudness changed, when the tune is the same; the two-star question tests exactly that. In
    the working, look for the child pointing along the strip and comparing each section with the
    first. The core reads the letters, the families and the marks from pp to ff, plays the start of
    Handel's own tune, and works back from a piece's length to the length of its B sections. The
    three-star question is a logic puzzle with four clues, easiest by starting with the brass.

    If the child is stuck, go back to the first question and trace each shape with a finger, saying
    its letter aloud; stay on this lesson another day if new instruments still get new letters. Off
    the page, listen to the Alla Hornpipe together (any recording from a library or a broadcast will
    do; the lesson plays no recording) and draw your own map as you listen: raise a hand when A
    comes back. The Water Music is from 1717 and long out of copyright. This lesson meets China's
    2022 arts standard for grades 3 to 5, 听赏与评述: "能听出音乐的主题…了解二段体、三段体、回旋曲式等音乐结构，并能用语言、动作、图示或乐谱等加以表示"
    (hear a piece's theme and show its form in words, movement, a diagram or a score).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the maps have five sections with A heard two or three times, one family count is worked in the teacher's pen before more, the loudness marks are p, mp, mf and f, the B sections come from pieces of 40 and 48 bars, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star asks about B, the section the woodwind clue settles."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the maps include a rondo and a piece with A heard four times, the families counted are the woodwind and the brass, the loudness marks run the whole way from pp to ff, the B sections come from pieces with shorter A sections, one more family question is asked, the two-star change is a slowing down rather than new instruments, and the three-star puzzle has its softest section moved."
  }
}
